course: dictionary
lesson: 4076
title: German: a language, a country, a people
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: German is a word with three jobs. It can name a language, as in «German class». It can describe something from Germany, as in «German bread». It can also name a person from Germany, a German. Look at the words around it to see which job it is doing.
explain: «Switzerland has four national languages, German, French, Italian and Romansh.» Here German is in a list of languages, so it names a language. The other words in the list are languages too, so the clue is easy to spot.
explain: «The German philosopher Immanuel Kant, in the 1700s, said you should never lie.» Here German comes before the job philosopher, so it tells you where Kant came from. Check by swapping in your meaning: a philosopher from Germany still makes sense, so German is describing him.
explain: Some German words have travelled into English. Kindergarten and hamburger are two of them. Another is hamstern, which in German means to hoard, and that is why a hamster's cheek pouches are so useful.
fact: German is the language of Germany.
fact: German is also spoken in Austria and Switzerland.
fact: A German is a person from Germany, and Germans are the people of Germany.
fact: Germany is in Europe, and its capital is Berlin.
fact: Kindergarten and hamburger are English words that came from German.
exercise: «Switzerland has four national languages, German, French, Italian and Romansh.» In this sentence, what does German mean? | A city in Germany | *A language | A kind of dog | A person from Germany
exercise: «The German philosopher Immanuel Kant said you should never lie.» What does German tell us about Kant? | That he was a hamster | *That he came from Germany | That he could not speak | That he was a famous dog
exercise: Which city is the capital of Germany? | *Berlin | Paris | Rome | Vienna
test: Kindergarten and hamburger are English words that came from which language? | Latin | Japanese | *German | Greek
test: In German, hamstern means what? | To sleep all day | *To hoard | To sing loudly | To run very fast
exam: In which sentence does German name a language? | «A German car drove past the school.» | «The German shepherd barked at the gate.» | «German bread is warm and tasty.» | *«I am learning German at school this year.»
